        title: 'Everything In The One Closet',
        body: 'Marisol was organised in a way her sisters teased her about, and the proof was the closet in the hall. Every important thing the family owned was in that closet, in labelled boxes, in order. The birth certificates. The passports. The deed. The marriage licence. Her father’s discharge papers. The external drive with every photograph taken since 2004, including the only pictures of her mother standing up. The spare keys to both cars and the lockbox with the emergency cash. Labelled. Alphabetised. In one closet, in one hallway, in one house. Her nephew, who was twenty-two and insufferable about exactly one subject, asked her over dinner what her plan was if the house burned. Marisol said the boxes were fireproof, and he asked if the hallway was, and she started to answer and then did not. It took her a Saturday. She scanned the documents and put them somewhere she could reach from a phone. She copied the drive and drove the copy forty minutes to her sister’s house, which is the same sister who teases her about the labels. She left the spare keys with a neighbour and moved half the cash. The closet is still labelled and still alphabetised, because she is who she is. It is just no longer the only place any of it exists, and she says that the strange part is how much lighter the hallway feels when you walk past it.',
