    lesson: 'Set the scene before the principle, because the emotional facts are carrying the teaching. Jacob is coming back into the land with everything he owns and everyone he loves, and word reaches him that the brother he defrauded twenty years ago is on the road to meet him with four hundred men. Scripture does not soften what that does to him. "Then Jacob was greatly afraid and distressed: and he divided the people that was with him, and the flocks, and herds, and the camels, into two bands;" (Genesis 32:7) Greatly afraid AND distressed — and then a verb of policy. DIVIDED. And the reasoning is not left for us to guess at, because he says it out loud: "And said, If Esau come to the one company, and smite it, then the other company which is left shall escape." (Genesis 32:8) Name exactly what he has done, because it is more careful than it first appears. Jacob has no influence at all over Esau’s intentions, and he knows it. What he can change is not the likelihood of the blow but its REACH — the proportion of everything he holds that one event is able to touch. That is the first risk division recorded in Scripture, and it is in substance the practice now called diversification: refusing to keep everything where a single bad day can reach all of it. Be equally clear about what it is not, because overselling it is how people end up disillusioned with it. The division does not improve Jacob’s standing with Esau. If Esau comes for both camps, both camps are lost. What the split does is bound the worst case, and that is a narrow claim and a true one, and narrow true claims are the only kind worth building a household on. Now the part most people need most, and it should not be hurried. In the same hour, between dividing the camp and meeting his brother, Jacob prays — and it is not the prayer of a man leaning on his own arrangement. "I am not worthy of the least of all the mercies, and of all the truth, which thou hast shewed unto thy servant; for with my staff I passed over this Jordan; and now I am become two bands." (Genesis 32:10) Read the final clause again, because it reframes the chapter: NOW I AM BECOME TWO BANDS. The two bands are not held up as Jacob’s cleverness. He counts them as evidence of mercy already received — he crossed that river once carrying a staff and nothing else, and what he has now is substantial enough that dividing it yields two camps. The same man who made the split accounts for the whole of it as a gift. So prudence and dependence are not in tension anywhere in Genesis 32, and any teaching that pits planning against faith has to get past this chapter first. It cannot. James states the ground that makes a division like this reasonable, and he states it flatly: "Whereas ye know not what shall be on the morrow. For what is your life? It is even a vapour, that appeareth for a little time, and then vanisheth away." (James 4:14) YE KNOW NOT WHAT SHALL BE ON THE MORROW. The ground is not fear and it is not pessimism about the world. It is ignorance of the future stated as simple fact. That is the honest position of every human being alive, and spreading exposure is the rational response to it rather than a symptom of weak faith — and notice that James does not answer the vapour with anxiety, he answers it with a sentence you are to say out loud: "For that ye ought to say, If the Lord will, we shall live, and do this, or that." (James 4:15) And Abram gives the military form of the same instinct when he goes after the raiders who carried off Lot: "And he divided himself against them, he and his servants, by night, and smote them, and pursued them unto Hobah, which is on the left hand of Damascus." (Genesis 14:15) Division as tactic there, division as protection here — the same refusal to present one undivided target. Now bring it into the actual house, which is where this session earns its place, because most people discover something uncomfortable in about ninety seconds. Name every asset a single event could reach at once. For an ordinary household the list runs: the building, everything inside it, the vehicle parked against it, the tools that produce the income, the paper records, the only copy of the photographs, the spare keys, and in a great many cases the only bank account and the only source of income. All of it inside one perimeter. One fire, one flood, one break-in, one bad night. Then ask where the second one is, and the silence that follows is the lesson. Two cautions as this closes, and they matter. This is not a licence for anxious multiplication. A household can spend years building redundancies it will never need and call that stewardship when it is really dread with a spreadsheet, and Yahweh has not asked anyone to live braced. And this is not a promise of safety — the next two lessons will make that painfully clear, one from a tower that fell on eighteen people and one from a man who lost everything in an afternoon. The claim of this session is narrow: you cannot govern what comes, and you can govern how much of what you hold a single arrival is able to take. So do the sheet. One line down the middle. On the left, everything one event could reach at once; on the right, where the second one is. Every blank on the right is something sitting in one band. Then pick the single blank whose blankness would hurt the most and fill in that one this week. One row. A plan that fixes nothing because it set out to fix everything is the most common failure in this entire subject.',
