    lesson: 'This session relocates the question, and the relocation is the entire work of it. Most households decide about cover out of one of two dispositions, and both of them decide badly. Fear buys whatever is offered, never reads the declarations page, pays too much for protection aimed at the wrong risk, and remains afraid afterward. Pride declines to look at all, tells itself the refusal is faith, and leaves people who depend on it exposed. Scripture does not put this question under either temperament. It puts it under PROVISION, and provision is a duty. "But if any provide not for his own, and specially for those of his own house, he hath denied the faith, and is worse than an infidel." (1 Timothy 5:8) Now state the limit before making any application, unprompted, because the integrity of this course depends on doing it that way round. Paul is writing about the care of relatives — widows in particular — inside a chapter about how the assembly orders that care. The passage does not mention insurance. It does not contemplate insurance. It cannot be made to command insurance, and anyone teaching it as a policy mandate is inflating the Word. An inflated verse loses its authority the first time a hearer goes and reads the chapter, and then the true thing underneath it is discredited along with the stretch. So take what the verse actually gives, which is a great deal: providing for your own household is a duty of FAITH rather than a feature of your personality, and the epistle speaks of failing it in the severest possible terms. Once that is settled, everything downstream changes shape. The question about a fire, a disability or an early death is no longer a measure of how anxious or how brave a person is. It becomes a plain question of stewardship: did you provide, for whom, and how would anyone be able to tell? Foresight itself does not need borrowed authority, because it is commended directly and without qualification. "A prudent man foreseeth the evil, and hideth himself: but the simple pass on, and are punished." (Proverbs 22:3) Put those two men beside each other. One sees what is coming and moves; the other proceeds as he was and is punished for it. Scripture calls the first PRUDENT, not faithless, and it is worth noticing that the same proverb appears a second time in the book — "A prudent man foreseeth the evil, and hideth himself; but the simple pass on, and are punished." (Proverbs 27:12) — which in Proverbs functions as emphasis. Now set the intended END of all this in front of the household, because the emotional register of the material matters as much as its content. "And my people shall dwell in a peaceable habitation, and in sure dwellings, and in quiet resting places;" (Isaiah 32:18) PEACEABLE. SURE. QUIET. That is what Yahweh names for His people, and it is the exact opposite of a family living braced. The verse before it grounds that peace in righteousness rather than in arrangements: "And the work of righteousness shall be peace; and the effect of righteousness quietness and assurance for ever." (Isaiah 32:17) A course like this one can easily manufacture vigilance and then call the vigilance stewardship. Vigilance is not the goal. REST is the goal. Provision exists to produce rest, never to substitute for it, and a household that has made its decisions and can sleep has got the point better than a household with a superior spreadsheet and a light on at two in the morning. Then hold the counterweight, with the same firmness, or this session tips over into self-reliance wearing Scripture. "Except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain that build it: except the LORD keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain." (Psalms 127:1) Read that carefully, because it is one of the most misquoted verses in this whole subject. It is routinely deployed as an argument against watching at all. It is not that. The watchman still wakes in the verse. The psalm’s claim is that his waking accomplishes nothing apart from Yahweh — not that he should go back to bed and leave the gate. So provision and dependence are not rivals, and the text most often used to excuse not providing turns out, read closely, to ASSUME that the watch is being kept. The household provides; He keeps. Both, in the same hour, exactly as Jacob prayed and divided in the same hour back in lesson three. Then make it concrete, because this is the session where a principle has to become a decision. The instrument is a named list, and the insistence on names rather than categories is the whole method. Write down who depends on your household. Not the kids — each child’s name. Not my mother — hers. Not the tenants — theirs. Then beside each name write what would specifically change for that person if the building burned, if the income stopped, or if you died this month. Be concrete to the point of discomfort: who moves out, who pays what, who takes the children to school, whose schooling ends, who has to take somebody in, who inherits an argument about paperwork they have never seen. Then circle the single name where the answer is worst. That name is the real subject of this course, and it tells you which decision your household should make FIRST, which is rarely the decision that is easiest to make. One last thing before you close the page, and say it gently to yourself. A household can do all of this faithfully and still suffer loss. Lesson four and lesson five already took that ground and this session does not walk any of it back. What provision decides is not whether loss comes; it is who carries the cost and how far the damage is allowed to reach. That decision is a duty. It is yours. And unlike almost everything else in this subject, it is makeable this month.',
