        title: 'The Nine Quiet Years',
        body: 'Hollis paid for flood cover for nine years on a house that had never flooded and, as far as anybody in that neighbourhood could remember, on a street that had never flooded, and every single year in the same week of March he said the same thing to his wife about throwing money in a hole. He was not being difficult. It was a real amount of money and it came out at a bad time of year and nothing ever happened. In the ninth year his brother-in-law told him he was being taken advantage of, and Hollis very nearly cancelled it; he had the page open. What stopped him was nothing noble. He got distracted by a phone call and then forgot for three weeks and by then the renewal had gone through. The water came in October of the following year, from a storm that stalled over the county for thirty-one hours, and it took the floors, the walls to eighteen inches, the furnace and everything in the crawlspace. He has told this story a number of times and he always tells it the same way, which is that he is not the hero of it. The nine quiet years were not the waste. The nine quiet years were the fence standing there doing its job while he complained about it every March.',
