        title: 'Two Numbers On One Line',
        body: 'I did the exercise in this lesson at a kitchen table with a man from our assembly who had asked me to look at his situation, and I want to report what we found because it was not what either of us expected. He was paying a genuinely significant amount every year across four different things, and he felt responsible about it, and he had been feeling responsible about it for a long time. We totalled it. Then I asked him the second question, which is what is the largest loss any of that is standing in front of, and we went through it one at a time. Three of the four were standing in front of things that would have been painful and survivable — a vehicle, some contents, a device plan he had forgotten he had. The fourth was nothing. He had no cover at all on the thing that actually produced his entire income, which was a trailer full of equipment that he towed to job sites and left overnight behind a chain-link fence. If that trailer went, he was finished, and he had been finished-adjacent for six years while diligently paying for a device plan. We fixed it in eleven days. What I took away, and what I say to people now, is that the two numbers have to be written on the same line. Not in your head. On a line. He was not careless and he was not proud. He had simply never seen the two numbers next to each other, and neither had I about my own house until I did it that night after I got home.',
