    lesson: 'Start with the emotional fact, because it is the real obstacle in this session and pretending otherwise wastes everyone’s time. A household pays month after month, for years, against something it hopes never arrives. Then the year closes and nothing happened, and the money feels wasted. That feeling is not stupid and it does not deserve a scolding. It is also the single most common reason a household cancels protection shortly before the year it needed it, which is why this lesson exists at all. So the task is to ANSWER the feeling rather than shame it. Name the mechanism plainly first. The regular payment a household makes so that a loss gets shared rather than landing entirely on it is called a premium, and there is nothing mysterious in it: it is Exodus 21:35 — sell the living ox, divide the money, divide the carcass — organised at scale and sold by the month. This course opened on that verse, and a premium is what pooling looks like when thousands of households do it together instead of two men at a field. Now go to a threshing floor, and read it carefully, because the worth of the passage depends entirely on not overstating it. David, after his sin and in the middle of a plague, is directed to build an altar on ground belonging to a man named Araunah. Araunah offers him the whole thing free — the floor, the oxen for the offering, and the wooden implements for fuel: "And Araunah said unto David, Let my lord the king take and offer up what seemeth good unto him: behold, here be oxen for burnt sacrifice, and threshing instruments and other instruments of the oxen for wood." (2 Samuel 24:22) The offer is generous and it is sincere, and the text notes the bearing with which it was made: "All these things did Araunah, as a king, give unto the king. And Araunah said unto the king, The LORD thy God accept thee." (2 Samuel 24:23) And David refuses, and gives his reason in a sentence that has long outlived its occasion. "And the king said unto Araunah, Nay; but I will surely buy it of thee at a price: neither will I offer burnt offerings unto the LORD my God of that which doth cost me nothing. So David bought the threshingfloor and the oxen for fifty shekels of silver." (2 Samuel 24:24) State the limit now, before drawing any application, exactly as this course does everywhere else. David is speaking about WORSHIP. The passage is not about insurance, it does not contemplate premiums, and it must not be dressed up as though it did. What David does state, and he states it as a principle rather than as a preference, is that a thing which has cost you nothing is worth nothing in your hand. Invert that and it lands squarely on the objection this lesson opened with. The cost is precisely what made the protection real. What your household bought in the quiet year was never an event; it was the STANDING not to be ruined — and it held that standing every day of that quiet year, including all the days it forgot it had it. A fence is not wasted because nothing escaped this year. The fence was working the entire year, and its working looks exactly like nothing happening. Learn that sentence, because you will need to say it to yourself in some February when the payment is due and the memory of the last fire has gone soft. Two more observations from the passage, and neither one is stretched. First, David pays a specific price and the text records the figure — fifty shekels of silver. That is the same instinct this course has returned to in every session: quantify it, write the number down, never leave the arrangement vague and verbal. Second, the chapter reports an outcome: "And David built there an altar unto the LORD, and offered burnt offerings and peace offerings. So the LORD was intreated for the land, and the plague was stayed from Israel." (2 Samuel 24:25) Do not turn that into a transaction. Yahweh was INTREATED; He was not purchased, and any teaching that makes fifty shekels the cause of His mercy has gone badly wrong and should be corrected on the spot. What is fair to say is that David’s refusal to bring what cost him nothing was the posture in which he came, and the chapter does not present that as incidental to what followed. Then run the instrument, which is two numbers on one line and which is far more revealing than it sounds. Total what your household actually pays across a full year for every form of protection it carries — all of it, as a single figure. Then write beside it the LARGEST loss that figure is standing in front of. Not the likeliest loss; people always reach for the likeliest and it teaches them nothing. The largest — the one that would actually end you. Now look at the two numbers on the same line. Most people find one of two mismatches, and both are extremely common. Either real money is being paid to stand in front of something that would merely be inconvenient, which is fear buying badly exactly as lesson six described it. Or the household is standing in front of something ruinous with almost nothing at all, which is pride declining to look. Neither of those can be diagnosed while the numbers live in separate places, and both are fixable within a month once they share a line. And close on the honest version of the promise, because this course will not oversell in its last sessions any more than in its first. Paying does not make a loss less likely by a single day. What paying does is decide, in advance and on purpose, how much of a loss your household will be made to carry alone. That decision is the thing being bought — and paying for it is what makes the decision yours rather than something the weather does to you.',
